  "body": "## Associated User Story\r\n\r\n[P5-38: Documentation and training](https://pt-502.atlassian.net/browse/P5-38)\r\n\r\nAs a user or officer, I would like documentation and training on how to use the app, so that I can use it and teach others without the dev team’s help.\r\n\r\nAs an officer, I would like documentation and training on how to install, run, and manage the system on Heroku, so that our org can maintain it without the dev team’s help.\r\n\r\n## Acceptance Criteria\r\n\r\n### Users and officers get app documentation and training with no Heroku or technical content, and officers get a separate system guide covering Heroku.\r\n\r\n- Sunny: A new user opens the help page and finds step-by-step instructions with screenshots for every feature. They use the app on their own without asking the dev team. A new officer does the same with the officer help page and learns every officer feature. The training matches the current website, covers all provided features, and uses visual aids, so both can train others afterward. Separately, an officer opens the system guide and follows it to install the app in the org’s Heroku account, find it on the dashboard, reach it at its URL, import existing data, and see what the subscription costs. The guide is written for non-developers, so the officers can set up and maintain the system without the dev team.\r\n- Rainy: A user or officer hits a common error, such as a failed login or a point submission that doesn’t go through, and finds the fix in the help page without contacting the dev team. A deploy breaks the app, and the admin follows the guide to roll back to the last working version on Heroku. A data import fails partway, and the guide shows how to see what didn’t transfer and retry without duplicating or corrupting data. Every help and training link goes to a live page, never a missing or dead one. After a website update, the docs are checked against the current site so screenshots and steps don’t go stale.\r\n\r\nDefinition of Done -\r\n\r\nA story is done when its acceptance criteria are met and every item in the team PR template is satisfied:\r\n(A) code review by a non-author, coding standard met, RuboCop checked with output/CI link, and setup changes documented;\r\n(B) the human-only maintainability checks (meaningful comments and names, skinny controllers, thin views, DRY, Rails conventions, foreign keys, enums, nested routes);\r\n(C) tests added or updated and passing. Any deferred items or RuboCop exclusions are explained in Section D of the PR.\r\n\r\n## Validation and Setup\r\n\r\nRSpec after updating from main: 338 examples, 0 failures, 1 existing pending example, including nine new examples for guide access, links/assets, and bootstrap retries. RuboCop, Rails loading, Brakeman, production assets, and desktop/phone checks passed. See the [PR checks](https://github.com/FA26-CSCE431-software-engineering/PT-502/pull/35/checks) for CI results.\r\n\r\nSetup changes, the Procfile, and initial-manager bootstrap instructions are documented in README and `docs/system-guide.html`.\r\n\r\nDepends on [#34](https://github.com/FA26-CSCE431-software-engineering/PT-502/pull/34): review and merge the profile changes first. This PR uses that branch as its base so its diff shows only P5-38.\r\n\r\n## Section D: Deferred Items\r\n\r\nNon-author code review and the human maintainability checks are awaiting team review. Live Heroku provisioning, import/restore, and rollback rehearsal remain owner tasks documented for staging. The existing pending HomeHelper example is unchanged. No new RuboCop exclusions.\r\n",
